HIV/Aids: China reports a 14% increase in new cases.
China has announced a 14% increase in the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 820,000 people are affected in the country, says health - care providers.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported only in the second quarter in 2018.
The vast majority of new cases were transmitted through sex, marking a change from the past.
Traditionally HIV scattered rapidly through some parts of China as a result of contaminated blood circulations.
However, the number of people receiving HIV in this way was reduced to nearly zero, said Chinese health professionals at a conference in Yunnan Province.
From year to year, however, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100,000 people.
HIV transmission through sex is an acute problem in China's LGBT environment.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination of LGBT/HBTQ characters are claimed to be widespread flore.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have counted that 70–90 percent of men who have sex with men will eventually marry women.
Many of the transmissions of diseases come from insufficient sexual protection in these relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has promised universal access to HIV medication as part of an effort to deal with the problem.
