Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed by a severe earthquake and a tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday says public officials.
Without the possibility of communicating, relief workers did not get any information from Donggala, an area north of Palu that is closer to the epicenter of the quake that had a magnitude of 7.5.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated in Palu after the catastrophe was a fact.
Here's a key fact about Palu and Donggala, lying on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital city of central Sulawesi Province, and is located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island.
The city celebrated its 40th Anniversary when the tsunami and the tsunami hit.
Donggala is an area stretching more than 200 miles (300 km) with a coastline in the north-west of the island.
The area, which is an administrative region lower than a provincial region, had an estimated population of 299,200.
Fishing and farming are the beams of the economy of central Sulawesi Province, especially in the Donggala coastal region.
nickel mining is important in the province, but is mostly concentrated in Morowali, on the other side of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been hit by tsunamis several times over the last 100 years, following the disaster reduction office in Indonesia.
In 1938, more than 200 people were killed and hundreds of houses were destroyed by a tsunami in Donggala.
In 1996 the west side of Donggala was struck by a tsunami that killed nine people.
Indonesia lies in the seismic Ring of the Pacific Ocean and is regularly affected by earthquakes.
Here's some of the big tremors and tsunamis in the last few years:
2004: A great quake on the west coast of the Aceh province of the northern Sumatra on 26 December triggered a tsunami that hit 14 countries and killed 226,000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than half of these in Aceh.
2005: A number of major tremors hit the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and early April.
Hundreds died on the island of Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A magnitude of magnitude 6.8 hit the south in Java, the most populous island in Indonesia, triggering a tsunami crashing into the southern coast and killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A magnitude of magnitude 7.6 occurred near the town of Padang, the capital of the province of West Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people lost their lives.
2010: A quake of magnitude 7.5 hit one of the Mentawai Islands outside Sumatra and triggered a tsunami of about 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A small quake struck the Pidie Jaya area of Aceh, causing devastation and panic when people were imminent the quake and tsunami in 2004.
No tsunami was triggered this time, but more than 100 lost their lives due to snatching buildings.
As of 2018, there has been a significant increase in the number of residents in the area, mostly on the northern side of the island.
The quake devastated several thousands of buildings and the aftermath of thousands of tourists midwide.
