Increasing on dinoflagellates: Concentration Rescues in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest report by the Florida Fisheries and Wildlife Commission (FWC) shows a general reduction in the concentration of dinoflagellates in Tampa Bay.
According to the FWC, there are reports of unmarried containment for the growth of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and the counties around Collier – suggest reduction in concentration.
The growth of dinoflagellates extends about 200 kilometres (200 mi) on the coastline from northern Pinellas to the southern counties of Lee.
Territories with dinoflagellates find about 10 miles (16 km) off the coast at Hillsborough, but compared to last week, there are fewer areas.
Dinoflagellates have been sighted in Pasco.
There have been reports of moderate concentrations in Pinellas over the past week, low to high concentrations along the Hillsborough coast, low concentrations in the Manatee, high concentrations along the coasts of Sarasota, medium concentrations in Charlotte, high concentration in Lee and low concentration in Collier.
Irritations in the respirators are still reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Irritations in the respirators were not reported in northwestern Florida in the last week.
